WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.434
-Welcome back
to "The Tonight Show."

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:03.601
-Thank you.
-This is interesting.

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:04.968
It's your first time
sitting here.

00:00:04.968 --> 00:00:06.467
-I've never sat on this couch.
-No, but you performed.

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:07.834
-It's comfy, yeah.
-It is comfy.

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:09.567
-Yeah, very nice.
-I'm so happy to see you

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:10.901
and you talk to you.
-Me too.

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:13.601
-Last time I saw you were --
it was a few months ago.

00:00:13.601 --> 00:00:15.467
You performed "Skinny Dipping."
-Yes.

00:00:15.467 --> 00:00:17.000
-Which was one of
my all-time jams.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.033
I love that song.
-You talked about it

00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:20.868
longer than I did.
-I was obsessed.

00:00:20.868 --> 00:00:22.868
-And I wrote it, yeah.
-I'm obsessed with that song.

00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:24.300
-Thank you.
-I love it so much.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.133
I love the -- I love
the conversation of it,

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:29.734
-The looseness of it.
-The first verse.

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:32.000
-That first verse, for me,
was the one I go, like,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.501
"What is this song?"
-Yeah.

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:36.400
-'Cause it kind of is like --
almost like, it sets the stage.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.167
It was almost like Paul Simony.
Can I play a little bit?

00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:39.868
-Oh, you have it on lock, yeah.

00:00:39.868 --> 00:00:41.501
♪ It'll be Wednesday ♪

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:43.968
♪ I'll be going
to this coffee shop ♪

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:46.734
♪ Hear the barista call an
oatmilk latte in your name ♪

00:00:46.734 --> 00:00:48.133
♪ And I look up from my phone ♪

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:51.234
♪ And think there's no chance
it's you, but it is ♪

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:52.400
♪ You'll say hi ♪
-All right, that's good.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.567
Say, "Hi. How are you?"
Yeah, I love that.

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:55.801
All right, we can stop.
But that's --

00:00:55.801 --> 00:00:57.167
Dude, I love how --
[ Applause ]

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:59.234
I go, "What is this song?"

00:00:59.234 --> 00:01:01.200
It felt like I was having
a conversation with someone.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.567
I loved it so much.
-That's the point.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.901
And it's supposed
to feel a little awkward.

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:05.534
'Cause, like, when you have
those, like, encounters

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:07.100
where you run into people
unexpectedly,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.434
and it's the worst
thing in the world

00:01:09.434 --> 00:01:12.033
but also the best
'cause it's life, you know?

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.501
It's really beautiful.
-And then you miss

00:01:13.501 --> 00:01:14.734
the good times and all --

00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:16.234
It turns into
the most beautiful song.

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:17.834
-Thanks.
-I love that.

00:01:17.834 --> 00:01:19.634
You also --
You confirmed that an album

00:01:19.634 --> 00:01:21.234
is coming out this year.
-Yes.

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:23.601
-But you haven't released
any details at all.

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:26.834
-I, actually -- It's funny.
Because I've hidden

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:29.367
the album name somewhere in work

00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:30.667
that I've put out
over the last year,

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.367
but my fans
haven't found it yet.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:33.501
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:01:33.501 --> 00:01:34.801
They haven't found it yet.
So...

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:35.968
-That's the new thing
people are doing.

00:01:35.968 --> 00:01:38.000
They, like -- you Easter egg
different things.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.467
And so, someone --
it's out there,

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:42.467
the name of the album.
-It is.

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.200
-Hmm. Does it have anything
to do with --

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.501
You sent me something,
a secret file yesterday,

00:01:46.501 --> 00:01:48.567
that I had to delete.
And it deleted after --

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:50.067
-Oh, I sent that to you?
-Yeah.

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:51.167
-Can you imagine?
I, like, didn't mean

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.267
to send it to you?
-Yeah.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:55.367
-Oh, no, yeah, I did.
And that would be

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:58.434
my new song...
-Yes.

00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:01.300
-...called...
-Can I say what it's called?

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.968
-Yeah.
[ Drumroll ]

00:02:02.968 --> 00:02:05.300
-"Fast Times"?
-It's called "Fast Times." Yeah.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:06.501
-I didn't know
if I could say it.

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:08.467
I'm like --
I'm living in a world where --

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.300
-I would so prefer you say it.
-I'm living in a world

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.200
where I'm afraid to say
what's gonna happen on

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.234
"This Is Us."
-I am, too.

00:02:13.234 --> 00:02:14.334
-I don't know
what's gonna happen.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.767
All of these Marvel movies.
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:17.100
-I don't know if I can
talk about your new song.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.634
-Yeah.
-"Fast Times," I love it.

00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.033
-Thank you.
-Thank you for sending it to me.

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:21.667
-Thank you. You liked it?
-I loved it.

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:23.133
As soon as I was done,
I wanted to hear it again.

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:24.834
And -- no.
-That's a good sign, yeah.

00:02:24.834 --> 00:02:26.501
-No, it's gone.
It left.

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:27.901
-Oh!
Oh, you could only listen once?

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:30.400
-Yeah, it deleted itself.
-Oh, I love my team.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.434
-Are there hidden things
in that song?

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:34.901
-You know, it's kind of funny.

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:38.267
I feel like it's something
that -- a few artists do it.

00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:41.534
And I think I just really
have had an amazing relationship

00:02:41.534 --> 00:02:45.000
with my fans over the years,
and they love being detectives.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.334
So, I like playing into it.
-Yeah.

00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:49.767
-Yeah, and then you can find
the name of my album,

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:51.367
and you can,
you know, announce it.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:53.367
'Cause it's out there.
-All right, I'm gonna find it.

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:55.000
-Yeah, that's your homework.
-All right, that's my homework.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.367
I'm gonna do it, yeah.
-Okay.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:58.000
-Do you record most of it
in New York?

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.167
-I did.
I kind of moved to New York

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:03.400
in hopes of finishing the record
just because, you know, like,

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.701
you do that thing
when you're 20,

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:06.200
and you're like,
"I have to move to New York."

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.000
-Of course.
-So I did it.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.200
And when I was here --

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.534
Obviously, the energy is just so
different from anywhere else.

00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:13.634
I grew up on the East Coast,

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:16.033
but New York
is a different beast.

00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:17.968
And there was one night
in particular --

00:03:17.968 --> 00:03:20.100
And this is how, like,
a song came to be.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.868
I was on my fire escape,
and my friend had a harmonica.

00:03:23.868 --> 00:03:25.467
And so I went out
and started playing harmonica,

00:03:25.467 --> 00:03:27.300
which I can't do very well.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.834
And there were was a lot of
drunk men down on the streets.

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:31.200
And they were like,
"Play us Billy Joel!"

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.334
And I was like,
"I can't do that."

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:35.701
I don't -- I'm not that good.
-They were asking for requests?

00:03:35.701 --> 00:03:37.434
-Yeah, song requests.
And --

00:03:37.434 --> 00:03:39.601
-Billy Joel.
-Yeah, which is, you know --

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:41.067
-Famous harmonica player --
-Exactly.

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:42.434
-Billy Joel, yeah.
-I was like,

00:03:42.434 --> 00:03:44.200
"I am not Billy Joel."
-Yeah, you don't have

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.400
a piano here but --
-No, my friend came out

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.634
with his guitar, and we were --
we grew, like,

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:50.701
a little audience for the night.
It was really fun.

00:03:50.701 --> 00:03:52.434
-That is so New York.
-It was so sweet, yeah.

00:03:52.434 --> 00:03:53.801
-That is exactly what you do.
-Yeah.

00:03:53.801 --> 00:03:55.200
-And how long
did the concert last?

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.734
-You know, we had two --
what do they call them?

00:03:57.734 --> 00:03:59.067
Stranglers?
Like, not "stranglers."

00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:00.567
Oh, my God, that's terrible.
Stragglers!

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:01.868
-Stragglers.
-Stragglers.

00:04:01.868 --> 00:04:03.567
-Who knows, who knows?
It's New York.

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:05.400
It's New York.
They could have been stranglers.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.000
[ Laughter ]
We don't know.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.167
You got to be careful.

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:09.767
Sabrina, it's your first time
in New York.

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:11.767
-My first time on your show,
too, obviously.

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:13.334
-Yeah, yeah.
No, they -- yeah.

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:14.567
They could've been stranglers,
yeah.

00:04:14.567 --> 00:04:16.334
So, I'm glad you're up
on the fire escape.

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:18.801
-Yeah, yeah. I was up.
-Away from the stranglers, yeah.

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:20.133
Can we talk about "Emergency"?

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:21.501
-Yes.
-This is giant.

00:04:21.501 --> 00:04:23.133
It's gonna premiere at Sundance.
-Yeah.

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:24.767
-Sundance Film Festival
is major.

00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:27.067
-Yeah, I --
It's kind of crazy saying it.

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.767
It's an amazing film.

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:31.734
It's based on a short film
that won at Sundance last year.

00:04:31.734 --> 00:04:35.234
And, basically, it's like
a dark comedy thriller,

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:37.701
but it touches on, you know,

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:40.534
just the fear
in our communities,

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:42.901
especially with young people of
color regarding the police.

00:04:42.901 --> 00:04:45.534
And, yeah, it's premiering
at the end of the month,

00:04:45.534 --> 00:04:46.868
and I'm so excited
for people to see it.

00:04:46.868 --> 00:04:50.300
-And this year, it's gonna be
also virtual, as well.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.834
So, January 20th,
look out for "Emergency."

00:04:52.834 --> 00:04:54.801
Thank you so much for coming on.
-Thank you.

00:04:54.801 --> 00:04:56.601
-And if you dropped
any Easter eggs --

00:04:56.601 --> 00:04:58.133
I don't know if you did --
-Can you imagine, like,

00:04:58.133 --> 00:04:59.267
throughout this interview,
I just said things?

00:04:59.267 --> 00:05:01.367
-Yeah.
We don't even know.

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.634
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:03.934
All right?
Get ready for it.

00:05:03.934 --> 00:05:05.667
[ Cheers and applause ]
[ Both laugh ]

00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:07.901
Sabrina Carpenter, "Emergency,"
will premiere

00:05:07.901 --> 00:05:10.801
at the Sundance Film Festival
on January 20th.

